    "body": "Time to make an issue for this. Here is the issue: \r\n\r\n* There are some patient records who we are functionally done with; either people who have elected to carry their pregnancies, or who have had their procedures with assistance from us, or who have just timed out past the point where they need our help (120 days or so).\r\n* We no longer really need a lot of their personally identifiable information (phone, name, etc) after this point.\r\n* We also may need to create another entry for them if they seek our services again in the future, which conflicts with our validation on unique phone. (Right now we have a hacky workaround of setting a clearly fake phone number.)\r\n* Thus, we need a way to 'archive' a patient to strip their information and get them out of rotation except for reporting. \r\n\r\nI think what this looks like from a technical perspective is wiping the phone numbers, setting an archive flag, and changing the name to a hash. A few caveats:\r\n\r\n* People with the same phone number and name should have the same hash, so we can still identify people who are second people;\r\n* Archiving should be irreversible.",
